<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Viscounty of Limoges</h1><p class="st-meta">876 CE – 1589 CE (approx.) · capital Limoges</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A viscounty in the Limousin that outlasted most feudal lordships and passed by inheritance to Brittany, Albret and finally the future Henry IV.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Viscounts appear at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> in the late 9th century as deputies of the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-poitou">Poitou</a>, who as dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a> claimed overlordship of the Limousin. The office became hereditary in a local family, which governed the city's castle quarter while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-martial">Saint-Martial</a> dominated the rest of the town.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The viscounts were middling lords caught between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">Plantagenet</a> kings and the French crown, often clashing with their own townsmen and with English overlords; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard the Lionheart</a> died in 1199 besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalus">Châlus</a>, a castle in the viscounty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> itself was known for its enamel work and as a pilgrimage centre on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Compostela</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Through marriage the viscounty passed to the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Brittany</a> in 1275, then to the Blois-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penthievre">Penthièvre</a> line and the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albret</a>. It reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Jeanne d'Albret</a>, and when her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV</a> became king in 1589 it was merged with the crown.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/viscounty-of-limoges.jpg" alt="Viscounty of Limoges" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Gerrito · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armes_Vicomtes_Limoges.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>876 CE – 1589 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Limoges</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arthur II of Brittany, Alain d'Albret, Henry IV of France</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Limousin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1232&amp;focus=viscounty-of-limoges" aria-label="Viscounty of Limoges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4710 563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Viscounty of Limoges lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5030 819L5035 888M5030 819L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5030 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5030 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Limoges. <a href="/atlas?year=1232&amp;focus=viscounty-of-limoges">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
